    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A delayed insurance claim in Corpus Christi can hold up roof repairs, keep a family out of its home, or leave a business closed while the bills keep coming. Delays happen for many reasons: adjuster turnover, catastrophe workloads after a major storm, repeated document requests, or a file that simply stopped moving. Texas law sets specific timelines for most property insurers, and TWIA has its own statutory schedule. Knowing those timelines, and keeping a clear written record, can help a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster or a policyholder push a stalled claim forward.</p>

<h2>Texas Prompt Payment Deadlines (Chapter 542)</h2>
<p>The Prompt Payment of Claims Act, Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, Subchapter B, applies to most property insurers. The <a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">statute</a> sets these general deadlines:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Statute</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin the investigation, and request needed items</td><td>15 days after notice of the claim (30 business days for eligible surplus lines insurers)</td><td>§542.055</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim</td><td>15 business days after receiving all required items (30 days if arson is suspected)</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>If more time is needed</td><td>The insurer must say why within that period, then decide within 45 days of that notice</td><td>§542.056</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay an accepted claim</td><td>5 business days after notice of acceptance (20 business days for surplus lines)</td><td>§542.057</td></tr>
<tr><td>Catastrophe extension</td><td>Deadlines extended 15 days after a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster, as defined by the Commissioner</td><td>§542.059(b)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Under §542.058, delay damages may apply when payment is delayed beyond the statutory period, or more than 60 days if no other statute sets a period. Whether those damages apply to a particular claim is a legal question; consult a Texas attorney.</p>
<p>One detail matters more than any other: the accept-or-reject clock generally runs from the date the insurer receives <em>all</em> the items it requested. That is why repeated document requests can extend a claim, and why written confirmation that the insurer has everything it needs is so valuable.</p>

<h2>TWIA Claims Follow a Separate Timeline</h2>
<p>Most coastal properties in Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties rely on the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association for wind and hail coverage. TWIA claims are governed by Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210, Subchapter L-1, which controls over conflicting law. Under the <a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.2210.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">statute</a>:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>TWIA stage</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Statute</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>File the claim</td><td>No later than one year after the date the damage occurs; the Commissioner may extend by up to 180 days for good cause</td><td>§2210.573(a), §2210.205</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA requests missing information</td><td>Within 30 days after the claim is filed</td><td>§2210.573(b)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA accepts in full, accepts in part, or denies</td><td>Within 60 days after receiving the claim or the requested information, whichever is later</td><td>§2210.573(d)</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA pays the accepted amount</td><td>Within 10 days after the notice (or after a required act by the claimant)</td><td>§2210.5731</td></tr>
<tr><td>Demand appraisal over the amount</td><td>Within 60 days after receiving the accept or partial-accept notice</td><td>§2210.574(b)</td></tr>
<tr><td>File suit over a denial</td><td>No later than two years after receiving the partial or full denial notice</td><td>§2210.577</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The Commissioner may extend TWIA's deadlines; extensions for TWIA-only deadlines on claims from one event are capped at 120 days total. Our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page and the article on the <a href="/twia-claim-process-corpus-christi/">TWIA claim process in Corpus Christi</a> explain the full process. To estimate the dates that may apply to your claim, use the <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a>. It is a planning tool, not legal advice.</p>

