<h2>Common Reasons Claims Stall</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Catastrophe volume.</strong> After hurricanes like Harvey or a widespread event like the May 8, 2025 windstorm, insurers bring in outside adjusters and files change hands.</li>
<li><strong>Rolling document requests.</strong> Each new request can reset the decision clock if the insurer treats the file as incomplete.</li>
<li><strong>Engineer or consultant reports</strong> that take weeks to schedule and complete.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple policies.</strong> Homeowners, windstorm, and flood insurers may each wait on the others to decide causation.</li>
<li><strong>Disputed scope</strong> that sits unanswered after a reinspection.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our article on <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas</a> covers these causes in more depth.</p>

<h2>What a Delay Can Cost You</h2>
<p>A slow claim is more than an inconvenience on the Texas coast. A roof that stays tarped through another storm can let in more water. Wet drywall and insulation that are not dried promptly can lead to mold, which many policies limit. Contractors book up after major storms, so a late decision can push repairs back by months. For coastal owners, a delay on one policy can also hold up another, because flood and windstorm insurers may each wait to see how the other handles causation.</p>
<p>That is why it helps to keep mitigating damage while you wait, save every receipt, and report any new damage in writing. A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can help you document conditions as they change, so later disputes about when damage occurred have a clear record to rely on.</p>

<h2>Practical Steps to Move a Delayed Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Put everything in writing.</strong> Follow up phone calls with an email summarizing what was said and what was promised.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim log</strong> with dates of notice, inspections, requests, and responses.</li>
<li><strong>Answer document requests completely and promptly,</strong> and ask the insurer to confirm in writing when it has all the items it needs.</li>
<li><strong>Ask for a specific decision date</strong> and the name of the adjuster responsible.</li>
<li><strong>Escalate within the insurer</strong> to a supervisor or claims manager if deadlines pass.</li>
<li><strong>Contact the Texas Department of Insurance.</strong> <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDI</a> accepts consumer complaints, and its ombudsman program helps TWIA policyholders.</li>
<li><strong>Talk with a Texas attorney</strong> if deadlines to sue may be approaching or you believe the law is being violated.</li>
</ol>
<p>Organized documentation also shortens the back-and-forth. The <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists the items insurers commonly request.</p>

<h2>How We Help With Delayed Claims</h2>
<p>We review the file to see where the claim actually stands: what the insurer has requested, what has been provided, and which deadlines may apply. Then we fill the gaps, providing a complete estimate, photos, and supporting records in one organized package so the insurer has what it needs to decide. We handle follow-up, track the timeline, and keep you informed. If the claim was accepted but paid too little, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>; if it was denied, see <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a>.</p>
<p>We cannot force an insurer to decide on a particular date or guarantee an outcome. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. If your claim has stalled, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
